WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.407 --> 00:00:11.012
- I'm delighted to see you
all and I'm actually thrilled

00:00:11.012 --> 00:00:13.811
that digital preservation would
bring so many people inside

00:00:13.811 --> 00:00:16.695
on such a nice day, it's very promising.

00:00:16.695 --> 00:00:18.371
I must say that I'm
quite honored to be here

00:00:18.371 --> 00:00:21.642
in this lecture series, because
following the two previous,

00:00:21.642 --> 00:00:22.704
Jim Reilly and Helen Shenton,

00:00:22.704 --> 00:00:24.760
who have made such
significant contributions

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:26.650
to the profession.

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:30.114
I wanna just make clear
that I don't see myself

00:00:30.114 --> 00:00:31.691
in that same role.

00:00:31.691 --> 00:00:34.493
Rather as someone who sort
of walks the border between

00:00:34.493 --> 00:00:36.694
people who use preserved information

00:00:36.694 --> 00:00:38.898
and people who actually
preserve the information.

00:00:38.898 --> 00:00:41.897
So I'm delighted to be
here in that capacity.

00:00:41.897 --> 00:00:43.730
I will not speak about preservation today

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:46.694
from a professional perspective,
because I am after all

00:00:46.694 --> 00:00:50.237
a historian, not a
preservationist, as Bobby said.

00:00:50.237 --> 00:00:53.415
Nor will I speak necessarily
from a personal point of view

00:00:53.415 --> 00:00:54.615
although I want to make clear

00:00:54.615 --> 00:00:57.301
that all the views that
I express are actually

00:00:57.301 --> 00:00:59.845
strictly my own and not
those of any organization

00:00:59.845 --> 00:01:01.803
with which I have worked.

00:01:01.803 --> 00:01:03.992
I also want to say that I'm going to speak

00:01:03.992 --> 00:01:05.257
in a very strong voice.

00:01:05.257 --> 00:01:08.062
I'm not going to be very
nuanced about what I say.

00:01:08.062 --> 00:01:11.685
So I imagine that this will
spark a number of questions.

00:01:11.685 --> 00:01:14.808
But I wanna just sweep
away some of the cobwebs

00:01:14.808 --> 00:01:17.697
in terms of general
understandings of preservation

00:01:17.697 --> 00:01:20.618
and of the specific role that
digital preservation plays

00:01:20.618 --> 00:01:22.606
in our culture today.

00:01:22.606 --> 00:01:24.122
Because that's what I want to talk about.

00:01:24.122 --> 00:01:27.561
I want to talk about preservation
from a social perspective.

00:01:27.561 --> 00:01:29.378
The role of preservation in culture

00:01:29.378 --> 00:01:32.452
and particularly our
culture at this moment,

00:01:32.452 --> 00:01:33.942
which I've characterized as

00:01:33.942 --> 00:01:36.615
the age of abundance of information.

00:01:36.615 --> 00:01:38.719
It's a period of information profligacy

00:01:38.719 --> 00:01:41.757
and I think actual promiscuity.

00:01:41.757 --> 00:01:44.107
But it also, it doesn't
mean that we have any more

00:01:44.107 --> 00:01:46.894
attention span, in fact the challenge is

00:01:46.894 --> 00:01:48.682
that we have more information

00:01:48.682 --> 00:01:52.272
and the same finite amount of attention.

00:01:52.272 --> 00:01:55.209
And I think this goes
very much to the thing

00:01:55.209 --> 00:01:56.419
that I want to talk about most,

00:01:56.419 --> 00:01:58.712
and that is the need to shape and select

00:01:58.712 --> 00:02:02.905
the historical record in a
way that we have not done

00:02:02.905 --> 00:02:04.822
in analog preservation.

00:02:05.761 --> 00:02:06.864
Not that we haven't done
it, but we've done it

00:02:06.864 --> 00:02:08.836
in a very different way.

00:02:08.836 --> 00:02:10.575
So I want to focus on the social context

00:02:10.575 --> 00:02:12.538
of digital preservation.

00:02:12.538 --> 00:02:15.503
Preservation of born digital materials.

00:02:15.503 --> 00:02:17.968
Most of all I want to
talk about the value,

00:02:17.968 --> 00:02:19.949
the value of preservation for society

00:02:19.949 --> 00:02:21.359
and so I will actually talk about

00:02:21.359 --> 00:02:24.185
the economics of information.

00:02:24.185 --> 00:02:26.738
I don't want you to be worried
I'm not an economist either

00:02:26.738 --> 00:02:28.915
and I'm not going to talk economies,

00:02:28.915 --> 00:02:31.052
but I have worked with
economists on this issue

00:02:31.052 --> 00:02:33.639
and they have a lot to tell us about value

00:02:33.639 --> 00:02:36.746
and how societies decide to
commit resources to one thing

00:02:36.746 --> 00:02:38.329
and not to another.

00:02:39.177 --> 00:02:42.378
What I say will apply in
part to analog materials

00:02:42.378 --> 00:02:44.195
that have been converted to digital.

00:02:44.195 --> 00:02:46.870
But I really want to address
the challenge of born digital

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:49.254
because it is the particular challenge

00:02:49.254 --> 00:02:51.915
that has been given to
us in this generation,

00:02:51.915 --> 00:02:54.684
whether we like it or not and
whether we trained for this.

00:02:54.684 --> 00:02:57.144
This is actually our challenge now

00:02:57.144 --> 00:02:59.218
and it's something that we
need to take very seriously

00:02:59.218 --> 00:03:03.388
because the clock is ticking
on a whole variety of fronts

00:03:03.388 --> 00:03:06.299
and it's not just the content
that we need to be preserving

00:03:06.299 --> 00:03:08.334
it's the professional skills
that we need to develop

00:03:08.334 --> 00:03:10.711
in order to deal with a
very changing environment

00:03:10.711 --> 00:03:14.400
of information, and changing
value of information.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.439
And by economics, I'm not
going to talk about cost models

00:03:17.439 --> 00:03:21.296
or business models or anything
having to do with capitalism

00:03:21.296 --> 00:03:22.733
or the free market.

00:03:22.733 --> 00:03:26.017
I mean simply that the
allocation of resources,

00:03:26.017 --> 00:03:29.669
which are always scarce, to
some things and not to another.

00:03:29.669 --> 00:03:32.511
So how is it that we
decide to put money here

00:03:32.511 --> 00:03:34.263
because it's always at the
expense of something else,

00:03:34.263 --> 00:03:35.218
or so we think.

00:03:35.218 --> 00:03:37.269
And so how do we actually
make those decisions?

00:03:37.269 --> 00:03:41.436
It's about judgements and
it's about our sense of value,

00:03:43.461 --> 00:03:47.019
because everything that
has value costs something,

00:03:47.019 --> 00:03:49.798
people, time and yes money.

00:03:49.798 --> 00:03:52.574
So where does preservation
fit into the list of things

00:03:52.574 --> 00:03:56.465
that our society deems most
important, if not essential?

00:03:56.465 --> 00:03:59.203
In other words, as I sometimes wonder,

00:03:59.203 --> 00:04:00.825
does anybody out there really get it,

00:04:00.825 --> 00:04:03.042
how important preservation is?

00:04:03.042 --> 00:04:05.765
And the answer to that
question is, yes definitely,

00:04:05.765 --> 00:04:07.097
they definitely do.

00:04:07.097 --> 00:04:10.547
People definitely get the value
of preservation in society.

00:04:10.547 --> 00:04:14.078
They have a special name
for it, they call it access.

00:04:14.078 --> 00:04:16.982
I think the problem from
preservation's point of view

00:04:16.982 --> 00:04:19.788
is that what they don't get is that

00:04:19.788 --> 00:04:22.989
there's a relationship between
preservation and access.

00:04:22.989 --> 00:04:25.711
That without preservation
there can be no access.

00:04:25.711 --> 00:04:27.601
And I think that there's a way that we,

00:04:27.601 --> 00:04:28.673
in the preservation community,

00:04:28.673 --> 00:04:30.751
and people who care about
the historical record,

00:04:30.751 --> 00:04:34.584
can, if not make them
care about preservation,

00:04:36.305 --> 00:04:38.962
for the process itself can
make them care very much

00:04:38.962 --> 00:04:40.757
about the outcomes of preservation,

00:04:40.757 --> 00:04:43.740
and therefore support
preservation as it should be.

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:47.715
I mean this is, this
lack of identification

00:04:47.715 --> 00:04:50.104
between access and preservation is acute

00:04:50.104 --> 00:04:53.983
in the digital realm, partly
because digital content is so,

00:04:53.983 --> 00:04:55.704
is not very long lived.

00:04:55.704 --> 00:04:56.905
And I mean that in many sense,

00:04:56.905 --> 00:04:58.711
there hasn't been information
or digital information

00:04:58.711 --> 00:05:01.376
for long and it certainly
doesn't last that long.

00:05:01.376 --> 00:05:05.019
But the failure I think goes
far deeper than the sort of,

00:05:05.019 --> 00:05:08.144
you know, what me worry storage is cheap,

00:05:08.144 --> 00:05:11.276
Alfred E Neuman school of preservation,

00:05:11.276 --> 00:05:15.209
which I've heard an astounding
number of technologists say.

00:05:15.209 --> 00:05:17.020
Technologists who really
should know better

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:19.333
because they've lost data.

00:05:19.333 --> 00:05:22.290
So I just, I want to say
just a word about the idea

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:25.841
of cheap storage, and this
is a very serious point.

00:05:25.841 --> 00:05:29.250
Storage requires energy and
we're not just talking about

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:31.284
digital storage, we're talking
about all forms of storage

00:05:31.284 --> 00:05:32.573
as you know.

00:05:32.573 --> 00:05:35.604
The use of power will never ever be cheap.

00:05:35.604 --> 00:05:38.431
I remember the days, I
miss the days actually,

00:05:38.431 --> 00:05:40.157
when experts would say that, you know,

00:05:40.157 --> 00:05:42.087
energy is actually not
going to be a problem

00:05:42.087 --> 00:05:43.765
because of nuclear power.

00:05:43.765 --> 00:05:46.158
And in fact nuclear power will be so cheap

00:05:46.158 --> 00:05:48.210
that we will stop metering it.

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:49.811
And I think that we're now living through

00:05:49.811 --> 00:05:52.341
an absolutely horrifying
catastrophe that shows

00:05:52.341 --> 00:05:55.836
just how expensive, not only energy is,

00:05:55.836 --> 00:05:57.695
and nuclear power in particular,

00:05:57.695 --> 00:06:01.056
but how incredibly dependent
we are on the power grid

00:06:01.056 --> 00:06:03.672
in ways that we can hardly calculate.

00:06:03.672 --> 00:06:06.491
I'm not saying that, you know,
we should go back to paper

00:06:06.491 --> 00:06:08.415
but I am saying that we need to attend to

00:06:08.415 --> 00:06:12.171
the entire spectrum of
issues that are related to

00:06:12.171 --> 00:06:14.336
digital storage and
the digital life cycle.

00:06:14.336 --> 00:06:16.127
It goes far beyond servers,

00:06:16.127 --> 00:06:18.113
it goes beyond the power grid itself.

00:06:18.113 --> 00:06:20.100
So the message about
preservation needs to be heard

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:21.468
at the very highest levels.

00:06:21.468 --> 00:06:24.260
It needs to be seen as
critical infrastructure

00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:26.093
which of course it is.

00:06:27.882 --> 00:06:29.942
I think more to the point the,

00:06:29.942 --> 00:06:32.203
it's cheap so we can store everything,

00:06:32.203 --> 00:06:34.401
misses the single most
important point which is

00:06:34.401 --> 00:06:36.667
not everything is equally valuable.

00:06:36.667 --> 00:06:39.470
It avoids the very tough
question of selection

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:41.135
which I think is absolutely critical

00:06:41.135 --> 00:06:43.427
and even more so in an age of abundance.

00:06:43.427 --> 00:06:46.496
And I know this is very
controversial in some areas

00:06:46.496 --> 00:06:48.639
and even in special
collections where people feel

00:06:48.639 --> 00:06:52.797
that selection is a way
of limiting and biasing

00:06:52.797 --> 00:06:55.400
the historical record, that it's difficult

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:57.639
and it is by definition, prejudicial.

00:06:57.639 --> 00:07:01.296
And I say, yes it is,
but we have no choice.

00:07:01.296 --> 00:07:03.586
In fact selection and collection building

00:07:03.586 --> 00:07:06.191
has always been culturally inflected.

00:07:06.191 --> 00:07:09.084
It has always reflected the
cultural biases of the people

00:07:09.084 --> 00:07:11.030
who create information
and the people who are

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:13.109
successive stewards of that.

00:07:13.109 --> 00:07:16.143
And I have to say as a
historian, you know I got that,

00:07:16.143 --> 00:07:18.653
I understood that the
historical record was not

00:07:18.653 --> 00:07:21.414
absolutely reflective of everything.

00:07:21.414 --> 00:07:24.601
That's why I went to school
to learn how to use resources

00:07:24.601 --> 00:07:27.234
and to have them tell
me what I wanted to hear

00:07:27.234 --> 00:07:31.117
rather than just rely on
the things that were there.

00:07:31.117 --> 00:07:33.557
And further more it makes
me believe that no matter

00:07:33.557 --> 00:07:37.184
what we leave to some extent,
the skill of interpretation

00:07:37.184 --> 00:07:38.989
lies with the interpretor.

00:07:38.989 --> 00:07:42.915
But we need to give them the
best possible perspective.

00:07:42.915 --> 00:07:44.903
The best possible choice.

00:07:44.903 --> 00:07:47.645
I will say also that having
studied for a long time

00:07:47.645 --> 00:07:50.191
in the USSR, I don't
believe that information

00:07:50.191 --> 00:07:51.483
wants to be free.

00:07:51.483 --> 00:07:53.063
I actually think that
the historical record

00:07:53.063 --> 00:07:56.246
is extremely important to
be conscious stewards of

00:07:56.246 --> 00:07:59.901
because information is very
easy to use and to manipulate

00:07:59.901 --> 00:08:03.647
for whatever purposes
you want, good or ill.

00:08:03.647 --> 00:08:06.189
And just one final point of introduction,

00:08:06.189 --> 00:08:07.528
those are my priors,

00:08:07.528 --> 00:08:09.657
before we start talking
about the good news

00:08:09.657 --> 00:08:12.810
about digital preservation as
well as some of the cautions.

00:08:12.810 --> 00:08:15.707
And that is what I know
is both as a historian

00:08:15.707 --> 00:08:17.976
because of the people
that I have worked with.

00:08:17.976 --> 00:08:20.498
I have spent many years working
in the Library of Congress

00:08:20.498 --> 00:08:22.097
and working with the Library of Congress

00:08:22.097 --> 00:08:25.257
and in many other places in
which I've just sponged up

00:08:25.257 --> 00:08:27.647
as much information as I could.

00:08:27.647 --> 00:08:29.919
And I think the thing that
I learned most importantly

00:08:29.919 --> 00:08:33.284
was what I saw the audio
visual preservationist

00:08:33.284 --> 00:08:35.752
grapple with at the Library of Congress.

00:08:35.752 --> 00:08:38.719
That audio visual, you're
already dealing with

00:08:38.719 --> 00:08:41.658
an historical record which
is extremely fragile.

00:08:41.658 --> 00:08:43.554
Which is already discounted historically

00:08:43.554 --> 00:08:46.499
as not significant although,
you know, we find out too late

00:08:46.499 --> 00:08:48.126
that it is significant.

00:08:48.126 --> 00:08:52.033
And so, also, how to deal
with things which are dynamic.

00:08:52.033 --> 00:08:54.304
That is they are dynamic in time.

00:08:54.304 --> 00:08:55.830
Because I think this is another challenge

00:08:55.830 --> 00:08:58.483
in digital preservation, is
a mind set that preservation

00:08:58.483 --> 00:09:01.424
is about fixing things once and for all.

00:09:01.424 --> 00:09:04.170
Having the right standards,
fixing the right version.

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:05.876
This is simply not going to be the case

00:09:05.876 --> 00:09:07.331
in digital preservation.

00:09:07.331 --> 00:09:10.369
So I think for those who
are shy about getting into

00:09:10.369 --> 00:09:13.364
digital preservation,
at least make friends

00:09:13.364 --> 00:09:15.570
with friends in audio,

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:18.198
with people in audio visual preservation

00:09:18.198 --> 00:09:21.352
because they have a lot
of this in their DNA.

00:09:21.352 --> 00:09:22.797
How to actually look at
this kind of material

00:09:22.797 --> 00:09:24.547
and assess its value.

00:09:26.665 --> 00:09:28.720
So I want to start with the good news

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:30.608
about digital preservation
because there has been

00:09:30.608 --> 00:09:34.156
an enormous amount of good news,
and it is mostly good news.

00:09:34.156 --> 00:09:35.897
It's amazing to think how far we've gotten

00:09:35.897 --> 00:09:37.980
in just 15 years roughly.

00:09:39.998 --> 00:09:42.836
First, in terms of awareness,

00:09:42.836 --> 00:09:45.030
the fact is that there are a
lot of people who are aware

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:47.285
of the need for digital preservation now.

00:09:47.285 --> 00:09:49.725
You could say that everyone
who's experienced some loss

00:09:49.725 --> 00:09:54.407
of data or having to upgrade
data understands that.

00:09:54.407 --> 00:09:57.433
There's also a huge number
of technical advances

00:09:57.433 --> 00:09:59.750
and I won't list them all
because there are so many

00:09:59.750 --> 00:10:02.805
but in terms of preservation
improved bit storage

00:10:02.805 --> 00:10:04.485
including the move to
the Cloud which I think

00:10:04.485 --> 00:10:06.137
is going to give us some kind of scale

00:10:06.137 --> 00:10:08.353
that we have longed for.

00:10:08.353 --> 00:10:10.917
Also better understanding of file formats

00:10:10.917 --> 00:10:13.065
and there has been enormous
amount of work done

00:10:13.065 --> 00:10:14.719
in preservation meta data.

00:10:14.719 --> 00:10:17.841
Really spectacular work
in preservation meta data.

00:10:17.841 --> 00:10:19.420
We're also seeing on the landscape

00:10:19.420 --> 00:10:23.566
increased institutional
commitments to digital preservation

00:10:23.566 --> 00:10:26.743
and I think that from our
perspective, in this library,

00:10:26.743 --> 00:10:28.530
that's the most significant.

00:10:28.530 --> 00:10:31.783
We've seen national libraries
taking responsibility

00:10:31.783 --> 00:10:33.436
for some domain.

00:10:33.436 --> 00:10:35.180
We see movie studios seriously investing

00:10:35.180 --> 00:10:36.713
in digital preservation.

00:10:36.713 --> 00:10:39.194
Financial companies have been
doing this for a long time.

00:10:39.194 --> 00:10:41.268
And there are a lot of
data archives, you know,

00:10:41.268 --> 00:10:43.430
it's not as if we had to
invent digital preservation

00:10:43.430 --> 00:10:45.453
for ourselves in the library world.

00:10:45.453 --> 00:10:47.829
Almost everything that
we want to know and use

00:10:47.829 --> 00:10:52.643
has been done already and
forged for us in another sector.

00:10:52.643 --> 00:10:54.839
Some institutions have even sprung up

00:10:54.839 --> 00:10:58.138
just to deal with preserving
the cultural heritage.

00:10:58.138 --> 00:11:00.213
I say some, but in fact
I have in mind one,

00:11:00.213 --> 00:11:01.874
which is the internet archive.

00:11:01.874 --> 00:11:05.291
The internet archive that started in 1996

00:11:06.298 --> 00:11:08.793
with a typical sort of know no bounds,

00:11:08.793 --> 00:11:10.493
I want everything approach.

00:11:10.493 --> 00:11:12.641
And it's been simply doing and learning,

00:11:12.641 --> 00:11:14.851
it's an iterative
approach, that its founder

00:11:14.851 --> 00:11:17.088
Brewster Kahle knows from the tech world.

00:11:17.088 --> 00:11:18.970
You got a problem, you try a solution.

00:11:18.970 --> 00:11:20.687
That doesn't work, you try it again.

00:11:20.687 --> 00:11:24.854
You never wait for version 3.0
because then it's too late.

00:11:25.736 --> 00:11:28.824
And I think that's very
hard for some library staff,

00:11:28.824 --> 00:11:30.284
library professionals to understand,

00:11:30.284 --> 00:11:33.701
coming from at least a paper based world.

00:11:34.731 --> 00:11:37.980
I think also the other
thing that's important is,

00:11:37.980 --> 00:11:41.979
on the issue of scale,
we've seen several models

00:11:41.979 --> 00:11:44.087
of shared stewardship be merged.

00:11:44.087 --> 00:11:46.664
I think everybody who knows the least bit

00:11:46.664 --> 00:11:50.824
about digital technology
knows that preserving data

00:11:50.824 --> 00:11:54.025
can't be done in the old
fashioned library way of,

00:11:54.025 --> 00:11:56.996
you know, one library one
campus in siloed collections.

00:11:56.996 --> 00:12:00.143
In fact there has to be a
scaled effort to build at least

00:12:00.143 --> 00:12:02.430
the infrastructure for
digital preservation.

00:12:02.430 --> 00:12:05.268
This has been forged by the
National Science Foundation

00:12:05.268 --> 00:12:08.385
when they put together the
Office of Cyber Infrastructure

00:12:08.385 --> 00:12:10.007
in which they felt that all of their

00:12:10.007 --> 00:12:12.930
extremely well funded preservation,

00:12:12.930 --> 00:12:16.282
sorry science directorates,
still could not tackle

00:12:16.282 --> 00:12:18.071
preservation on their own.

00:12:18.071 --> 00:12:21.078
Or the building of the
infrastructure it had to be done

00:12:21.078 --> 00:12:23.742
at scale which meant across institutions

00:12:23.742 --> 00:12:25.659
and disciplinary lines.

00:12:27.698 --> 00:12:31.507
I want to touch briefly on some bad news

00:12:31.507 --> 00:12:34.445
which is, which is
essentially all of the above.

00:12:34.445 --> 00:12:37.070
Everything that I said
has some down side to it,

00:12:37.070 --> 00:12:38.473
not surprisingly.

00:12:38.473 --> 00:12:40.459
So in the area of technical advances

00:12:40.459 --> 00:12:44.262
we've made enormous strides
in preservation advances.

00:12:44.262 --> 00:12:47.544
But we have not kept pace
with the technical advances

00:12:47.544 --> 00:12:49.745
in the creation of information.

00:12:49.745 --> 00:12:53.197
In fact we're falling very far behind.

00:12:53.197 --> 00:12:54.898
There's not much we can do about that

00:12:54.898 --> 00:12:58.219
but that is the case that
the ability to create data

00:12:58.219 --> 00:13:01.098
will always outstrip our
ability to preserve it.

00:13:01.098 --> 00:13:03.907
We have already technically far acceded

00:13:03.907 --> 00:13:05.049
the amount of information there is,

00:13:05.049 --> 00:13:08.164
has far acceded the storage
capacity world wide.

00:13:08.164 --> 00:13:10.199
And this is just of scientific data.

00:13:10.199 --> 00:13:12.136
So even those people who were power users

00:13:12.136 --> 00:13:17.049
are beginning to understand
that they can't save everything.

00:13:17.049 --> 00:13:18.178
I want to say about awareness,

00:13:18.178 --> 00:13:19.641
I think there is a great deal of awareness

00:13:19.641 --> 00:13:22.348
in the cultural heritage
institutions about

00:13:22.348 --> 00:13:24.134
the need for digital preservation.

00:13:24.134 --> 00:13:26.406
But we see a lot of institutions saying,

00:13:26.406 --> 00:13:28.042
but that doesn't mean me.

00:13:28.042 --> 00:13:30.712
That doesn't mean me because
I don't have the capacity.

00:13:30.712 --> 00:13:33.921
I don't have the funding,
I don't have the expertise.

00:13:33.921 --> 00:13:36.398
And I think the saddest cases in my mind

00:13:36.398 --> 00:13:39.739
are historical societies
and small regional libraries

00:13:39.739 --> 00:13:42.549
and in the major
metropolitan public libraries

00:13:42.549 --> 00:13:44.528
which have a very valuable role

00:13:44.528 --> 00:13:47.315
in collecting regional history

00:13:47.315 --> 00:13:49.813
not just serving the public best sellers.

00:13:49.813 --> 00:13:51.967
And I think that they're really bereft,

00:13:51.967 --> 00:13:54.304
they're really left on
their own in this world

00:13:54.304 --> 00:13:57.425
and so that's why I think the
idea of shared stewardship

00:13:57.425 --> 00:14:00.536
is even more important to
build a robust infrastructure

00:14:00.536 --> 00:14:03.270
that small institutions can use almost as

00:14:03.270 --> 00:14:04.998
a kind of service bureau.

00:14:04.998 --> 00:14:07.210
And I think the preservation
world has really modeled

00:14:07.210 --> 00:14:10.494
this extremely well in the field services

00:14:10.494 --> 00:14:12.578
that they provide in some of the,

00:14:12.578 --> 00:14:16.409
some of the service bureaus
that they have for preservation

00:14:16.409 --> 00:14:18.811
that serve very small constituents.

00:14:18.811 --> 00:14:21.460
After all we know that
local history in the micro

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:22.960
is very important.

00:14:24.215 --> 00:14:27.994
Now I wanna speak slightly
against the grain here

00:14:27.994 --> 00:14:29.474
and talk about the bad news in terms of

00:14:29.474 --> 00:14:31.554
institutional commitments
because I talked about

00:14:31.554 --> 00:14:35.676
national libraries, financial
companies, movie studios.

00:14:35.676 --> 00:14:38.416
I want to know where the
ARL libraries are in this

00:14:38.416 --> 00:14:41.231
and I'm not talking
about converted materials

00:14:41.231 --> 00:14:43.880
when they've done a
spectacular job of converting

00:14:43.880 --> 00:14:46.321
their materials for access purposes.

00:14:46.321 --> 00:14:49.419
Nor am I talking about the
extensive work that has been done

00:14:49.419 --> 00:14:53.782
in negotiating access
to scholarly literature

00:14:53.782 --> 00:14:55.260
which is born digital,

00:14:55.260 --> 00:14:58.730
access which has a preservation
back end in many cases.

00:14:58.730 --> 00:15:00.441
There's a kind of, if
you go out of business,

00:15:00.441 --> 00:15:01.711
we get to keep this.

00:15:01.711 --> 00:15:04.571
I'm talking about the
born digital material

00:15:04.571 --> 00:15:06.771
that the humanist of the
future will be using.

00:15:06.771 --> 00:15:09.115
Not scholarly literature
but the primary sources

00:15:09.115 --> 00:15:10.365
of our culture.

00:15:11.974 --> 00:15:15.539
I think to a surprising degree
I think research libraries

00:15:15.539 --> 00:15:18.688
whose specialty actually
is that have been really

00:15:18.688 --> 00:15:20.561
missing in action and I think that

00:15:20.561 --> 00:15:23.810
there's a significant
risk that this will go on

00:15:23.810 --> 00:15:26.946
for some time if libraries
don't just step in

00:15:26.946 --> 00:15:29.988
and try to do it, and
do just a little bit.

00:15:29.988 --> 00:15:31.345
You don't have to do much.

00:15:31.345 --> 00:15:33.775
But work with your
special collections area.

00:15:33.775 --> 00:15:35.976
You know what your
collecting strengths are

00:15:35.976 --> 00:15:38.075
just go out and do some web archiving.

00:15:38.075 --> 00:15:40.592
Or something else, partner with people

00:15:40.592 --> 00:15:42.197
who are digital creators because

00:15:42.197 --> 00:15:45.660
there's a lot of opportunity
there for your feet to get wet

00:15:45.660 --> 00:15:48.376
in a kind of no fault area.

00:15:48.376 --> 00:15:51.655
I think that from an
economic point of view

00:15:51.655 --> 00:15:53.646
there might be very compelling reasons why

00:15:53.646 --> 00:15:55.979
research libraries are among

00:15:57.340 --> 00:15:59.068
the last to enter into the space.

00:15:59.068 --> 00:16:02.253
Not among the last but
certainly likert I'd say

00:16:02.253 --> 00:16:04.934
considering the importance
of these institutions.

00:16:04.934 --> 00:16:06.421
And it is, as I learned from economists

00:16:06.421 --> 00:16:08.995
on the Blue Ribbon Task Force,
it's an economic reason.

00:16:08.995 --> 00:16:10.882
It's because, to some extent,

00:16:10.882 --> 00:16:13.459
libraries have lost their
incentive to preserve.

00:16:13.459 --> 00:16:15.085
And I know that sounds counter-intuitive

00:16:15.085 --> 00:16:17.625
because why would a
library need an incentive

00:16:17.625 --> 00:16:20.216
if in fact it's their mission to preserve.

00:16:20.216 --> 00:16:23.403
But I think that sometimes
missions are lip service

00:16:23.403 --> 00:16:24.967
and that you can have a mission

00:16:24.967 --> 00:16:26.716
that you're not able to fulfill.

00:16:26.716 --> 00:16:28.561
And it still means a great deal to you

00:16:28.561 --> 00:16:30.262
but it's not something which is actionable

00:16:30.262 --> 00:16:32.637
that you will actually
dedicate a lot of money to.

00:16:32.637 --> 00:16:34.974
And I think preservation
is at risk of becoming this

00:16:34.974 --> 00:16:36.714
in the digital world.

00:16:36.714 --> 00:16:39.349
I think the incentive
for libraries to preserve

00:16:39.349 --> 00:16:42.773
analog collections has been access.

00:16:42.773 --> 00:16:45.028
You could not serve
readers without providing

00:16:45.028 --> 00:16:46.502
a book for them.

00:16:46.502 --> 00:16:48.874
And you could not serve this faculty

00:16:48.874 --> 00:16:52.206
without having books that
dated from 1850 or before.

00:16:52.206 --> 00:16:54.596
It was simply something
that you had to do.

00:16:54.596 --> 00:16:56.859
Libraries weren't founded
around preservation

00:16:56.859 --> 00:16:59.098
they were founded around
access and preservation

00:16:59.098 --> 00:17:02.135
was built as a back end to support access

00:17:02.135 --> 00:17:03.718
that people wanted.

00:17:04.589 --> 00:17:08.455
You know the country has been
very slow at a policy level

00:17:08.455 --> 00:17:11.445
to move in step with the digital realm.

00:17:11.445 --> 00:17:14.043
The copyright law,
despite recommendation for

00:17:14.043 --> 00:17:16.895
tiny incremental changes to section 108,

00:17:16.895 --> 00:17:20.809
to allow copying of digital
materials for libraries.

00:17:20.809 --> 00:17:24.976
That law has not been amended,
our Congress seems to be

00:17:25.811 --> 00:17:29.379
quite distracted by a
number of other things.

00:17:29.379 --> 00:17:33.157
I'd say in the meantime, the
good news is, in the meantime

00:17:33.157 --> 00:17:35.784
copyright law has become quite irrelevant

00:17:35.784 --> 00:17:38.563
to a large number of people
who are creating data.

00:17:38.563 --> 00:17:42.041
And I think that gives
us a real opportunity.

00:17:42.041 --> 00:17:45.091
I don't think that as long
as materials are licensed,

00:17:45.091 --> 00:17:47.409
we deal with the licensing regime

00:17:47.409 --> 00:17:49.579
as opposed to the copyright law,

00:17:49.579 --> 00:17:52.600
we're going to be doing
significantly differently

00:17:52.600 --> 00:17:55.009
than we are now, and I think
we've made considerable strides

00:17:55.009 --> 00:17:56.447
in that area.

00:17:56.447 --> 00:17:57.280
But there's a lot of,

00:17:57.280 --> 00:17:59.301
even in the area of scholarly literature,

00:17:59.301 --> 00:18:01.421
there's an increasing
amount of scholarship

00:18:01.421 --> 00:18:03.686
which is written outside of journals

00:18:03.686 --> 00:18:04.999
which is very important.

00:18:04.999 --> 00:18:06.942
There are lots of scholarly
blogs in certain fields

00:18:06.942 --> 00:18:09.880
where actually, which are
quite preservation worthy

00:18:09.880 --> 00:18:13.463
and are looking for
homes for preservation.

00:18:16.452 --> 00:18:19.619
I wanna talk also about the good news

00:18:19.619 --> 00:18:23.808
of shared stewardship
models because in fact,

00:18:23.808 --> 00:18:26.960
I mean I talked about the
shared stewardship model

00:18:26.960 --> 00:18:29.310
as a good thing, and it is.

00:18:29.310 --> 00:18:31.737
Actually there have been
a large number that say,

00:18:31.737 --> 00:18:33.976
ENDIP is one, Program
and Locks is another,

00:18:33.976 --> 00:18:36.699
and HathiTrust is yet a third.

00:18:36.699 --> 00:18:38.655
But particularly when it
comes to universities,

00:18:38.655 --> 00:18:43.238
there's a downside to that
which is sharing is not easy

00:18:43.238 --> 00:18:46.063
and universities are,
particularly the ones

00:18:46.063 --> 00:18:47.984
that have the richest collections,

00:18:47.984 --> 00:18:50.856
there in a very competitive
stance, one with another.

00:18:50.856 --> 00:18:53.318
They compete for students,
they compete for faculty,

00:18:53.318 --> 00:18:56.551
they compete for collections,
they compete at sports.

00:18:56.551 --> 00:19:00.016
And I think that this
is very much out of step

00:19:00.016 --> 00:19:02.062
with what's needed in the digital world.

00:19:02.062 --> 00:19:04.583
I do believe that in fact
there are universities

00:19:04.583 --> 00:19:07.922
talking about building shared
preservation infrastructure

00:19:07.922 --> 00:19:10.456
because they've encountered
already that this is not

00:19:10.456 --> 00:19:13.142
something that they can
do except collectively.

00:19:13.142 --> 00:19:14.887
I would say Locks is an example of that,

00:19:14.887 --> 00:19:17.155
HathiTrust is an example of that.

00:19:17.155 --> 00:19:19.798
But I think it's a very serious issue

00:19:19.798 --> 00:19:22.018
which needs to be addressed
in the next couple of years

00:19:22.018 --> 00:19:26.185
and I expect Yale will be
part of the solution for that.

00:19:27.612 --> 00:19:31.059
And be eager to join, I mean,
even Harvard has announced

00:19:31.059 --> 00:19:33.313
that it's willing to join

00:19:33.313 --> 00:19:37.133
in some aspects of sharing
collections and preservation.

00:19:37.133 --> 00:19:40.831
So I would say there's
a fairly good example

00:19:40.831 --> 00:19:44.248
up in the brick campus up north.

00:19:44.248 --> 00:19:46.246
I want to spend the rest of my time here

00:19:46.246 --> 00:19:48.666
talking specifically
about some of the findings

00:19:48.666 --> 00:19:50.279
from the Blue Ribbon Task Force

00:19:50.279 --> 00:19:54.913
on sustainable digital
preservation and access.

00:19:54.913 --> 00:19:59.618
It's in part because I think
this is work that tends to be,

00:19:59.618 --> 00:20:01.981
that hasn't gotten a
lot of traction because

00:20:01.981 --> 00:20:04.625
it looks like it's about
economics and I think

00:20:04.625 --> 00:20:06.586
that that scares a lot of people away.

00:20:06.586 --> 00:20:08.525
But I think it's actually a salubrious way

00:20:08.525 --> 00:20:11.016
of looking at the challenges
of building support

00:20:11.016 --> 00:20:13.433
for preservation because in the end,

00:20:13.433 --> 00:20:15.617
preservation has to pay its way.

00:20:15.617 --> 00:20:17.162
It doesn't have to pay its way financially

00:20:17.162 --> 00:20:19.843
but it has to be something
that society says

00:20:19.843 --> 00:20:23.216
it is willing to invest in
as opposed to something else

00:20:23.216 --> 00:20:27.047
and I think we can make a
very good case for that.

00:20:27.047 --> 00:20:28.994
Several years ago the
National Science Foundation

00:20:28.994 --> 00:20:32.744
and the Library Congress
got together to put,

00:20:33.945 --> 00:20:36.732
it wanted to get
preservationists and economists,

00:20:36.732 --> 00:20:38.910
you know, academic
economists, real economists,

00:20:38.910 --> 00:20:40.944
to talk about the fundamental economics

00:20:40.944 --> 00:20:42.882
of digital sustainability.

00:20:42.882 --> 00:20:46.686
Why, because digital
preservation had been dealt with

00:20:46.686 --> 00:20:49.532
as a technical problem,
and as legal problem,

00:20:49.532 --> 00:20:52.002
and as a collecting
problem, but never seriously

00:20:52.002 --> 00:20:53.591
as an economic problem.

00:20:53.591 --> 00:20:56.109
And I'll say economists do
not think of what they do

00:20:56.109 --> 00:21:00.670
as business, business
models and cost models,

00:21:00.670 --> 00:21:02.732
that's actually what people
in business school do

00:21:02.732 --> 00:21:05.763
as they told me on the task force.

00:21:05.763 --> 00:21:07.582
They actually look at society's engagement

00:21:07.582 --> 00:21:08.687
with the commitment of resources

00:21:08.687 --> 00:21:10.243
to things that they care about.

00:21:10.243 --> 00:21:13.711
We looked at it from a
very, a global perspective.

00:21:13.711 --> 00:21:17.246
We had, we worked for two
years, we produced two reports.

00:21:17.246 --> 00:21:20.041
The first report was purely descriptive.

00:21:20.041 --> 00:21:22.507
It just looked at the world
and said what have we done,

00:21:22.507 --> 00:21:25.376
what solutions are there
for digital preservation

00:21:25.376 --> 00:21:28.878
and paying for it, and what
lessons have been learned.

00:21:28.878 --> 00:21:30.258
What are the things that people have tried

00:21:30.258 --> 00:21:33.360
that have not succeeded
that we should not try.

00:21:33.360 --> 00:21:36.085
The second year was
what we call normative,

00:21:36.085 --> 00:21:38.035
looking at where we're going,
or actually in this case,

00:21:38.035 --> 00:21:41.059
looking at where we wanted to
go, and then plotting a course

00:21:41.059 --> 00:21:45.918
for how we got, how we
would get there from here.

00:21:45.918 --> 00:21:48.533
It did sometimes feel like, that you know,

00:21:48.533 --> 00:21:50.095
we were maining.

00:21:50.095 --> 00:21:51.389
The person would just say
well we can't get there

00:21:51.389 --> 00:21:52.603
from here you know.

00:21:52.603 --> 00:21:56.904
But in fact there is a way
to engage all the sectors

00:21:56.904 --> 00:22:01.059
that we need in economic
support of this activity.

00:22:01.059 --> 00:22:03.614
In some ways simply by
re-calibrating how we describe

00:22:03.614 --> 00:22:05.886
what we do, and addressing
a different audience

00:22:05.886 --> 00:22:06.969
than we have.

00:22:11.838 --> 00:22:16.210
The economist did a very
good job of characterizing

00:22:16.210 --> 00:22:18.668
preservation as an economic activity

00:22:18.668 --> 00:22:21.545
and I won't give your their economies.

00:22:21.545 --> 00:22:25.378
It's incredibly funny
and amusing to hear them

00:22:26.744 --> 00:22:29.743
describe preservation in economic terms.

00:22:29.743 --> 00:22:31.399
They have an array of technical terms

00:22:31.399 --> 00:22:33.553
which are really quite
interesting and get at

00:22:33.553 --> 00:22:37.414
some very core concepts but,
as the writer of the report,

00:22:37.414 --> 00:22:40.443
I found that they needed
translation constantly

00:22:40.443 --> 00:22:43.547
and so I will speak to you
in translated economies,

00:22:43.547 --> 00:22:45.956
and tell you that for digital preservation

00:22:45.956 --> 00:22:47.675
we determined that there were

00:22:47.675 --> 00:22:50.042
five conditions for sustainability

00:22:50.042 --> 00:22:52.849
and there are only five conditions.

00:22:52.849 --> 00:22:54.406
This is all incidentally in the report

00:22:54.406 --> 00:22:56.103
and I will tell you how to find the report

00:22:56.103 --> 00:22:58.020
at the end of the talk.

00:22:59.458 --> 00:23:00.767
I'm not going to give you a URL,

00:23:00.767 --> 00:23:02.821
I will give you the title
again so you can google it.

00:23:02.821 --> 00:23:05.149
The URL is a little ungainly.

00:23:05.149 --> 00:23:07.957
So the five conditions for support are,

00:23:07.957 --> 00:23:10.106
a recognition of the
benefits of preservation

00:23:10.106 --> 00:23:11.588
by decision makers.

00:23:11.588 --> 00:23:13.440
And a decision maker is
anybody who is in a position

00:23:13.440 --> 00:23:17.089
to commit a resource to something,

00:23:17.089 --> 00:23:19.934
or to influence a decision I should say.

00:23:19.934 --> 00:23:21.915
Who is a decision maker at Yale?

00:23:21.915 --> 00:23:24.797
Well obviously the President
is, the head of the library is.

00:23:24.797 --> 00:23:26.555
Anybody who runs a budget is.

00:23:26.555 --> 00:23:28.925
But the faculty are also decision makers.

00:23:28.925 --> 00:23:33.092
They can set priorities for
what's important on campus.

00:23:34.147 --> 00:23:35.584
So but the important thing is to recognize

00:23:35.584 --> 00:23:37.819
the benefits of preservation.

00:23:37.819 --> 00:23:40.574
That is, what is it that stakeholders get

00:23:40.574 --> 00:23:41.773
out of preservation?

00:23:41.773 --> 00:23:43.694
Again it's about the outcomes.

00:23:43.694 --> 00:23:45.384
The second thing that's
needed is a process

00:23:45.384 --> 00:23:48.060
for selecting digital
materials with long term value.

00:23:48.060 --> 00:23:50.899
That's very very difficult
and I think people found it

00:23:50.899 --> 00:23:52.585
the most difficult task.

00:23:52.585 --> 00:23:56.502
But there was absolutely
no interest in anybody

00:23:57.977 --> 00:24:01.285
from the task force
coming out and saying that

00:24:01.285 --> 00:24:03.632
we should be keeping
everything because we can.

00:24:03.632 --> 00:24:05.525
It was very clear to
the people who put this,

00:24:05.525 --> 00:24:07.268
who charged the task force,

00:24:07.268 --> 00:24:10.758
that in fact presidents of
universities for example

00:24:10.758 --> 00:24:12.798
would not invest in digital preservation

00:24:12.798 --> 00:24:14.384
if they thought it was a black hole

00:24:14.384 --> 00:24:16.651
and it would be collecting
everything indiscriminately.

00:24:16.651 --> 00:24:18.931
They wanted a lot of work
going into a selection

00:24:18.931 --> 00:24:21.817
which I think is very smart.

00:24:21.817 --> 00:24:22.913
There have to be incentives,

00:24:22.913 --> 00:24:25.114
the third is there have to be
incentives for decision makers

00:24:25.114 --> 00:24:28.099
to make the decision to
preserve in the public interest.

00:24:28.099 --> 00:24:30.626
And I will talk a little
bit about incentives

00:24:30.626 --> 00:24:32.266
because it seems like a sort
of odd thing to talk about

00:24:32.266 --> 00:24:36.266
in preservation and odd
in terms of mission work

00:24:37.531 --> 00:24:39.440
but in fact incentives are key to actually

00:24:39.440 --> 00:24:41.243
getting things done.

00:24:41.243 --> 00:24:42.868
There needs to be, the fourth is,

00:24:42.868 --> 00:24:45.562
there needs to be appropriate
organization and governance

00:24:45.562 --> 00:24:47.861
of digital preservation activities.

00:24:47.861 --> 00:24:51.092
And I'll say that I'd be
surprised if this campus

00:24:51.092 --> 00:24:54.080
were any different than any
campus I've ever been on

00:24:54.080 --> 00:24:57.180
where digital preservation
happens in multiple places

00:24:57.180 --> 00:24:59.130
and nobody knows where they are.

00:24:59.130 --> 00:25:01.137
Nobody knows what other people are doing

00:25:01.137 --> 00:25:03.216
and they don't even know about
the duplication of effort

00:25:03.216 --> 00:25:04.223
that they're making.

00:25:04.223 --> 00:25:07.151
So it is really critical
that there be a strategy

00:25:07.151 --> 00:25:08.876
for digital preservation

00:25:08.876 --> 00:25:10.769
and that people know what the strategy is

00:25:10.769 --> 00:25:13.274
and there be clear lines of responsibility

00:25:13.274 --> 00:25:15.571
for who does what.

00:25:15.571 --> 00:25:19.250
We heard from many government agencies

00:25:19.250 --> 00:25:23.714
that produce public records
that, you know, are by law

00:25:23.714 --> 00:25:25.148
to be preserved.

00:25:25.148 --> 00:25:28.963
Somebody was ready to hand
off a record, a digital record

00:25:28.963 --> 00:25:31.190
for example, be he a
scientist in a laboratory

00:25:31.190 --> 00:25:33.440
or somebody in immigration.

00:25:35.290 --> 00:25:38.033
They would turn to somebody
and say so where do I send this

00:25:38.033 --> 00:25:41.251
and there was no answer,
nobody knew who was in charge.

00:25:41.251 --> 00:25:43.551
And so I think that it's
quite essential that people

00:25:43.551 --> 00:25:47.240
be tasked specifically and that, second,

00:25:47.240 --> 00:25:50.167
that everybody knows who
is tasked specifically

00:25:50.167 --> 00:25:53.020
about who is in charge of what.

00:25:53.020 --> 00:25:56.933
It's the only way to actually
implement the strategy

00:25:56.933 --> 00:25:58.518
because it is after all quite,

00:25:58.518 --> 00:26:01.451
we're dealing with a femoral
and distributed materials.

00:26:01.451 --> 00:26:03.216
It's not as if somebody can just put

00:26:03.216 --> 00:26:04.832
a whole bunch of books in a room and say,

00:26:04.832 --> 00:26:06.862
okay, let's take care of this.

00:26:06.862 --> 00:26:10.400
It has to be handled in a distributed way.

00:26:10.400 --> 00:26:14.364
The fifth challenge is the
mechanisms for securing

00:26:14.364 --> 00:26:17.318
ongoing and efficient
allocation of resources

00:26:17.318 --> 00:26:20.289
to digital preservation activities.

00:26:20.289 --> 00:26:22.386
Efficient doesn't mean cheap it means

00:26:22.386 --> 00:26:24.158
that you get the biggest
bang for your bucks.

00:26:24.158 --> 00:26:26.506
So there's an appropriate
amount of redundancy

00:26:26.506 --> 00:26:30.598
but there's not unnecessary duplication.

00:26:30.598 --> 00:26:34.222
Not surprisingly the
things that people found

00:26:34.222 --> 00:26:37.552
the greatest challenge about
meeting those conditions.

00:26:37.552 --> 00:26:39.534
I shouldn't say, incidentally,
that the first year

00:26:39.534 --> 00:26:41.906
we spent looking for
somebody who could claim

00:26:41.906 --> 00:26:44.302
that they have fulfilled
all five of those conditions

00:26:44.302 --> 00:26:46.436
and the closest we got
was somebody who thought

00:26:46.436 --> 00:26:50.348
they were doing, oh, sort
of three, maybe four.

00:26:50.348 --> 00:26:52.592
But then on closer examination
we asked them questions

00:26:52.592 --> 00:26:55.087
and decided no they weren't
actually doing that many either.

00:26:55.087 --> 00:26:56.926
So it's complicated and it's not as if

00:26:56.926 --> 00:27:00.086
there's a model out there
that people can hand off

00:27:00.086 --> 00:27:01.850
to somebody else.

00:27:01.850 --> 00:27:03.745
Getting into conversation,
I think, with people,

00:27:03.745 --> 00:27:05.711
other people who are doing
digital preservation,

00:27:05.711 --> 00:27:08.952
is possibly the first intelligent step.

00:27:08.952 --> 00:27:11.323
But almost in order of the challenges,

00:27:11.323 --> 00:27:13.894
I'll tell you what they were.

00:27:13.894 --> 00:27:16.247
The first was uncertainty
about selection criteria

00:27:16.247 --> 00:27:19.358
which I mentioned, for
assessing long term value.

00:27:19.358 --> 00:27:21.882
On the one hand we had
scientists talking about

00:27:21.882 --> 00:27:23.679
large scale data sets.

00:27:23.679 --> 00:27:27.535
Some of them could say
well we know that those,

00:27:27.535 --> 00:27:30.310
you know, those petabytes
of data from space,

00:27:30.310 --> 00:27:32.635
will not be useful after
10 years because, you know,

00:27:32.635 --> 00:27:34.536
we'll have superseded
the observational data

00:27:34.536 --> 00:27:35.815
with something else.

00:27:35.815 --> 00:27:37.869
On the other hand there was a
different set of astronomers

00:27:37.869 --> 00:27:41.619
who said you know what,
all astronomical data

00:27:42.718 --> 00:27:44.048
is historical data.

00:27:44.048 --> 00:27:46.041
We can't get rid of anything.

00:27:46.041 --> 00:27:48.258
So they were having a hard
time sorting through that one

00:27:48.258 --> 00:27:50.270
but they did realize they
had to make a choice.

00:27:50.270 --> 00:27:53.090
So they were thinking about
ways to sample historical data

00:27:53.090 --> 00:27:55.367
so that they could do it cheaply.

00:27:55.367 --> 00:27:57.010
We also found a great challenge

00:27:57.010 --> 00:28:00.710
with the small handcrafted
digital collections,

00:28:00.710 --> 00:28:03.696
many of which are bound on
campuses made by students

00:28:03.696 --> 00:28:05.808
and faculty members.

00:28:05.808 --> 00:28:10.536
And then the biggest one which
we decided we couldn't avoid

00:28:10.536 --> 00:28:13.557
is the plethora of new
genres that are emerging

00:28:13.557 --> 00:28:16.557
on the web, mediated by social media

00:28:17.468 --> 00:28:19.791
and the so-called open web.

00:28:19.791 --> 00:28:21.991
Things like Wikipedia and Twitter.

00:28:21.991 --> 00:28:26.064
Things that we know are of
enormous social significance

00:28:26.064 --> 00:28:28.923
but what part of it is significant?

00:28:28.923 --> 00:28:30.728
How we collect it, how we save it,

00:28:30.728 --> 00:28:35.393
what are the copyright and
ownership claims to it?

00:28:35.393 --> 00:28:38.260
Not a clue, absolutely not a clue.

00:28:38.260 --> 00:28:42.478
I think it was on the
basis of the complete gulf

00:28:42.478 --> 00:28:45.228
about how important it was versus

00:28:46.160 --> 00:28:47.556
knowing what to do about it,

00:28:47.556 --> 00:28:50.150
that really prompted the
Library of Congress to say

00:28:50.150 --> 00:28:53.251
you know, we're going to
take the Twitter archive.

00:28:53.251 --> 00:28:54.694
We don't know what that means,

00:28:54.694 --> 00:28:58.173
we have no idea what that means
but Twitter doesn't either

00:28:58.173 --> 00:29:02.444
and who better to find
out with than Twitter.

00:29:02.444 --> 00:29:06.125
So that's the kind of partnership
where you can go into it

00:29:06.125 --> 00:29:10.292
with an expectation of
failure but it's a failure,

00:29:11.740 --> 00:29:14.411
but failures are learning experiences.

00:29:14.411 --> 00:29:16.864
If it's failure without
a learning experience

00:29:16.864 --> 00:29:18.506
then it is really a failure.

00:29:18.506 --> 00:29:20.571
But if you go into something knowing that

00:29:20.571 --> 00:29:23.178
the outcome actually is knowledge,

00:29:23.178 --> 00:29:25.297
not necessarily saving something,

00:29:25.297 --> 00:29:27.709
then you can get pretty
far with trying out

00:29:27.709 --> 00:29:30.609
different methods for selection.

00:29:30.609 --> 00:29:34.265
I can tell you my
colleagues at the library

00:29:34.265 --> 00:29:38.432
were absolutely gobsmacked
when the first public reaction

00:29:40.290 --> 00:29:42.351
we heard some people
saying, hey that's great,

00:29:42.351 --> 00:29:44.827
were people individually trying to reach

00:29:44.827 --> 00:29:47.505
individuals in the Library
of Congress saying,

00:29:47.505 --> 00:29:51.079
that Twitter, I want my
Twitter back, you know.

00:29:51.079 --> 00:29:54.383
I didn't know that it was
going to be preserved for ever.

00:29:54.383 --> 00:29:56.781
I never knew that it was,
that anyone had a right

00:29:56.781 --> 00:29:57.614
to preserve it.

00:29:57.614 --> 00:30:01.585
And of course it's all
very clear on the Twitter

00:30:01.585 --> 00:30:03.986
when you click through the licence that,

00:30:03.986 --> 00:30:06.379
unless you make an exemption,
you know, it is actually,

00:30:06.379 --> 00:30:08.005
it's not your private property any more.

00:30:08.005 --> 00:30:12.479
And people publish on line
and it's totally accessible

00:30:12.479 --> 00:30:15.547
but they're very sensitive
about it being preserved.

00:30:15.547 --> 00:30:18.427
So you run into some
very unexpected issues

00:30:18.427 --> 00:30:20.760
simply by trying things out.

00:30:22.410 --> 00:30:25.856
There's also another
challenge which I'll get into.

00:30:25.856 --> 00:30:28.214
It's quite specific in every community.

00:30:28.214 --> 00:30:29.981
The scholarly community
or scientific community

00:30:29.981 --> 00:30:32.198
or Hollywood for example.

00:30:32.198 --> 00:30:34.258
But there's a really
fundamental misalignment

00:30:34.258 --> 00:30:36.343
of incentives to preserve.

00:30:36.343 --> 00:30:39.811
Copyright law is completely
out of whack with

00:30:39.811 --> 00:30:42.518
what we need to be doing in
terms of copyrighted material

00:30:42.518 --> 00:30:45.351
and the rewards in the academy for

00:30:46.870 --> 00:30:50.290
attending to digital preservation
where it matters the most

00:30:50.290 --> 00:30:52.123
are also out of whack.

00:30:53.141 --> 00:30:58.048
And I will actually spend some
time on talking about that.

00:30:58.048 --> 00:31:00.264
And then finally I want
to say that another thing

00:31:00.264 --> 00:31:01.780
that was really baffling
which I don't think

00:31:01.780 --> 00:31:05.845
we're ever going to solve is
trying to discriminate between

00:31:05.845 --> 00:31:08.405
preservation, the cost of
making something accessible,

00:31:08.405 --> 00:31:12.122
versus the cost of making
something, preserving something.

00:31:12.122 --> 00:31:14.054
In fact they're not separable,

00:31:14.054 --> 00:31:16.545
and so teasing out preservation
costs as opposed to

00:31:16.545 --> 00:31:19.304
access costs is a fool's game.

00:31:19.304 --> 00:31:22.767
I think it's much better to
thing of long term sustained

00:31:22.767 --> 00:31:25.852
data management in which
you look at every object

00:31:25.852 --> 00:31:28.118
as having a lifecycle.

00:31:28.118 --> 00:31:31.927
And the lifecycle can be five
years, it can be one year

00:31:31.927 --> 00:31:35.085
and it can be 50 years
but it's much more useful

00:31:35.085 --> 00:31:38.023
to think about lifecycle
costs and lifecycle management

00:31:38.023 --> 00:31:39.435
of digital data.

00:31:39.435 --> 00:31:40.875
And it also makes it a whole lot easier

00:31:40.875 --> 00:31:44.042
to think about selection in this case.

00:31:46.046 --> 00:31:49.634
The really good news is we
did find that once the risks

00:31:49.634 --> 00:31:51.692
were identified and we
recommended that everybody

00:31:51.692 --> 00:31:53.928
doing an assessment of
where they are with this,

00:31:53.928 --> 00:31:56.585
do a self assessment of where they are

00:31:56.585 --> 00:31:59.383
based on some of the things in our report.

00:31:59.383 --> 00:32:01.868
We found that in fact
for nearly everything

00:32:01.868 --> 00:32:05.653
that we thought of as a
risk, there is a mitigation.

00:32:05.653 --> 00:32:07.863
There is actually a lot to be done

00:32:07.863 --> 00:32:10.250
in order to make the environment friendly

00:32:10.250 --> 00:32:12.368
for digital preservation.

00:32:12.368 --> 00:32:13.981
And the really good news I think is that

00:32:13.981 --> 00:32:15.771
it is everybody's problem.

00:32:15.771 --> 00:32:19.118
It's not Yale's problem
and it's not my problem.

00:32:19.118 --> 00:32:21.460
It's actually everybody's problem.

00:32:21.460 --> 00:32:25.191
So when rumor first spread
that the Library of Congress

00:32:25.191 --> 00:32:28.053
and NSF were looking to put
together this task force

00:32:28.053 --> 00:32:31.415
on economics we had a flood
of people volunteering

00:32:31.415 --> 00:32:34.923
time and effort and money, and
a lot of interest on the part

00:32:34.923 --> 00:32:37.945
of people in a university administration.

00:32:37.945 --> 00:32:39.607
Of course they also thought
that we would hand them

00:32:39.607 --> 00:32:42.329
a blueprint for what is to be done.

00:32:42.329 --> 00:32:46.355
Actually a business model as
opposed to an economic model.

00:32:46.355 --> 00:32:48.654
But I think economic models are,

00:32:48.654 --> 00:32:50.741
business models are
actually pointless without

00:32:50.741 --> 00:32:51.958
an economic model in.

00:32:51.958 --> 00:32:54.271
We did get fairly far to
articulating several types

00:32:54.271 --> 00:32:57.595
of economic models, some for profit

00:32:57.595 --> 00:33:01.762
and for the four profit sector,
and some not for profit.

00:33:02.849 --> 00:33:05.140
So the three things that I want to say

00:33:05.140 --> 00:33:07.429
as the final recommendations which I think

00:33:07.429 --> 00:33:10.326
are most important for this community are,

00:33:10.326 --> 00:33:12.326
and they are just three.

00:33:15.391 --> 00:33:17.282
They're not easy.

00:33:17.282 --> 00:33:19.505
Is first and foremost to
articulate a compelling

00:33:19.505 --> 00:33:23.074
value proposition for preservation.

00:33:23.074 --> 00:33:26.209
I said first that in the
beginning that people

00:33:26.209 --> 00:33:28.088
really do care about preservation.

00:33:28.088 --> 00:33:31.551
They just don't call it
preservation, they call it access.

00:33:31.551 --> 00:33:34.383
I think that particularly
in the preservation field,

00:33:34.383 --> 00:33:36.572
if you really want to get more resources,

00:33:36.572 --> 00:33:38.931
and the right kind of resources for work,

00:33:38.931 --> 00:33:41.595
start talking about the
outcomes of preservation.

00:33:41.595 --> 00:33:43.265
Start talking about the
things that you can do

00:33:43.265 --> 00:33:46.434
with preserved assets, or
however you want to call them.

00:33:46.434 --> 00:33:49.324
In the scientific world they
can talk about the promise

00:33:49.324 --> 00:33:51.932
of future productivity of science.

00:33:51.932 --> 00:33:54.232
How do we talk about that in a library?

00:33:54.232 --> 00:33:57.124
How do we actually talk
about the future productivity

00:33:57.124 --> 00:33:58.649
of our students and our faculty?

00:33:58.649 --> 00:34:00.668
Well we talk about it
actually all the time

00:34:00.668 --> 00:34:01.988
though without the collections,

00:34:01.988 --> 00:34:04.695
research and teaching
is really engendered.

00:34:04.695 --> 00:34:07.856
So talk first about what
the outcomes of preservation

00:34:07.856 --> 00:34:09.669
are going to be, and then talk about

00:34:09.669 --> 00:34:12.295
what it takes to get there from here.

00:34:12.295 --> 00:34:15.206
The value of proposition
of saving astronomical data

00:34:15.206 --> 00:34:18.311
is going to be different
from saving feature films

00:34:18.311 --> 00:34:21.094
for example, but every audience has

00:34:21.094 --> 00:34:22.850
it's own sort of sweet spot in terms of

00:34:22.850 --> 00:34:24.745
what it really wants to pay attention to.

00:34:24.745 --> 00:34:26.497
But I think I would start first with,

00:34:26.497 --> 00:34:28.948
what is the benefit of preservation?

00:34:28.948 --> 00:34:30.686
That's the recognition of benefit

00:34:30.686 --> 00:34:33.062
on the part of stakeholders.

00:34:33.062 --> 00:34:36.511
The second thing is to provide
clear incentives to preserve

00:34:36.511 --> 00:34:38.428
in the public interest.

00:34:40.287 --> 00:34:42.895
What does that mean to provide incentives

00:34:42.895 --> 00:34:45.693
and clear incentives to
preserve in the public interest?

00:34:45.693 --> 00:34:47.737
Well providing incentives
for stakeholders,

00:34:47.737 --> 00:34:49.394
as we say anybody who
cares about the outcome

00:34:49.394 --> 00:34:52.023
of preservation, to preserve
digital assets to directly,

00:34:52.023 --> 00:34:55.050
or to provide preservation
services for others

00:34:55.050 --> 00:34:57.341
and tailoring those incentives
to the prevailing community

00:34:57.341 --> 00:34:59.911
norms and to information
policy regulations

00:34:59.911 --> 00:35:03.800
and privacy concerns, is really
what we're talking about.

00:35:03.800 --> 00:35:06.830
We're talking about both
creating incentives,

00:35:06.830 --> 00:35:08.847
positive things that
get people to preserve

00:35:08.847 --> 00:35:12.865
and also removing disincentives,
of which there are many.

00:35:12.865 --> 00:35:15.940
To take a fairly simple example,

00:35:15.940 --> 00:35:18.723
I'll take a bench scientist
at Yale University

00:35:18.723 --> 00:35:21.156
or a medical scientist at Yale.

00:35:21.156 --> 00:35:25.323
One incentive that a scientist
has to preserve digital data

00:35:27.442 --> 00:35:29.857
is the possibility of reusing the data

00:35:29.857 --> 00:35:32.857
if in fact they're interested
in sharing the data.

00:35:32.857 --> 00:35:36.633
A much stronger incentive
would be if his grant

00:35:36.633 --> 00:35:39.058
depended upon digital curation.

00:35:39.058 --> 00:35:41.352
If the grant actually
stipulated that the data

00:35:41.352 --> 00:35:45.127
had to be managed and archived
and that digital curation

00:35:45.127 --> 00:35:46.532
was part of the grant,

00:35:46.532 --> 00:35:48.486
and there was money in the grant for it.

00:35:48.486 --> 00:35:50.137
And there would be a penalty to be paid

00:35:50.137 --> 00:35:52.441
if the curation didn't happen.

00:35:52.441 --> 00:35:55.122
Now that's really an incentive.

00:35:55.122 --> 00:35:56.990
It's one thing for NSF to say,

00:35:56.990 --> 00:35:58.420
everybody needs to save their data

00:35:58.420 --> 00:36:01.962
and curate their data,
but that's not enough

00:36:01.962 --> 00:36:04.030
because I can't think of,

00:36:04.030 --> 00:36:06.443
we actually asked people at
the Foundation and they said,

00:36:06.443 --> 00:36:10.236
well privately they didn't
know of any particular grant

00:36:10.236 --> 00:36:13.704
which had been denied a PI
because they hadn't saved data

00:36:13.704 --> 00:36:15.594
from a previous grant.

00:36:15.594 --> 00:36:17.065
They gave them a hard time maybe

00:36:17.065 --> 00:36:19.210
but they didn't actually
deny research funds.

00:36:19.210 --> 00:36:21.582
Research funds is what
researchers care about

00:36:21.582 --> 00:36:24.184
so holding back money
would be very important.

00:36:24.184 --> 00:36:26.725
A big advance is that NSF is now saying

00:36:26.725 --> 00:36:29.561
the people have to have
a data management plan.

00:36:29.561 --> 00:36:30.891
That's very good, nobody knows what

00:36:30.891 --> 00:36:33.615
a data management plan is yet.

00:36:33.615 --> 00:36:36.573
But I see this as an opportunity
for libraries frankly.

00:36:36.573 --> 00:36:37.743
I mean how many of you have gone over

00:36:37.743 --> 00:36:41.105
to science laboratories and say,

00:36:41.105 --> 00:36:43.713
hey you got a data management
plan that you're worried about

00:36:43.713 --> 00:36:45.139
maybe I can help you.

00:36:45.139 --> 00:36:46.987
This is really an opportunity to begin

00:36:46.987 --> 00:36:50.674
data curation where it
counts which is at the bench.

00:36:50.674 --> 00:36:53.062
I'd also say that there
are positive disincentives

00:36:53.062 --> 00:36:55.319
for scientists to curate data.

00:36:55.319 --> 00:36:56.338
And what are they?

00:36:56.338 --> 00:37:01.040
Well because every second,
every penny that's not spent

00:37:01.040 --> 00:37:05.536
on research is really a
demerit in his portfolio

00:37:05.536 --> 00:37:08.424
because there is no reward
in his reward system

00:37:08.424 --> 00:37:11.401
or her reward system for
curating and preserving data.

00:37:11.401 --> 00:37:14.187
It's just time away from
something that is rewarded.

00:37:14.187 --> 00:37:16.994
So I think the incentives
are really out of skew

00:37:16.994 --> 00:37:18.994
within the university itself.

00:37:18.994 --> 00:37:22.316
That on the one hand you
reward people for creating data

00:37:22.316 --> 00:37:24.976
on the other hand there's
a positive disincentive

00:37:24.976 --> 00:37:27.399
for people preserving data
and somehow they think that

00:37:27.399 --> 00:37:30.661
all of, somehow things are
going to end up in the library

00:37:30.661 --> 00:37:33.462
sort of magically packaged and
ready for you to take care of

00:37:33.462 --> 00:37:34.801
and serve up again.

00:37:34.801 --> 00:37:37.282
This is a fundamental misunderstanding.

00:37:37.282 --> 00:37:40.622
Data curation really
begins with the creator

00:37:40.622 --> 00:37:43.973
and it should be in
partnership with librarians,

00:37:43.973 --> 00:37:46.889
preservationists, data scientists,

00:37:46.889 --> 00:37:49.425
informatics specialists,
whatever you want to call them.

00:37:49.425 --> 00:37:51.156
Every discipline will
have a different name.

00:37:51.156 --> 00:37:54.940
But it's a partnership that
begins at the very beginning.

00:37:54.940 --> 00:37:59.289
I think that libraries
have, well I should say,

00:37:59.289 --> 00:38:02.409
universities have a second opportunity

00:38:02.409 --> 00:38:07.235
as well as a responsibility
and that is, you have,

00:38:07.235 --> 00:38:10.321
thousands of students coming
through here every year.

00:38:10.321 --> 00:38:12.496
You teach them literacy in many ways.

00:38:12.496 --> 00:38:17.004
Media literacy, reading
literacy, mathematical literacy.

00:38:17.004 --> 00:38:19.758
Digital literacy is what is needed now

00:38:19.758 --> 00:38:23.901
because our students are digital,
they've grown up digital.

00:38:23.901 --> 00:38:26.560
They know how to use machines fluently,

00:38:26.560 --> 00:38:29.480
many times better than
we do we think, we fear.

00:38:29.480 --> 00:38:31.040
But that doesn't mean that
they're literate necessarily,

00:38:31.040 --> 00:38:33.495
and they know how to use information well.

00:38:33.495 --> 00:38:36.491
There's a chance that,
as well as an obligation,

00:38:36.491 --> 00:38:39.413
that we can take the current
generation of sort of

00:38:39.413 --> 00:38:43.853
web 2.0ers, sort of read
write culture of technology,

00:38:43.853 --> 00:38:48.020
and turn it into a read, write,
curate and preserve culture.

00:38:49.024 --> 00:38:50.805
I think that everybody who goes through

00:38:50.805 --> 00:38:53.687
an undergraduate program
at any good university

00:38:53.687 --> 00:38:56.694
or college, where actually it
should start in high school.

00:38:56.694 --> 00:39:00.158
People should be taught how
to manage their own data

00:39:00.158 --> 00:39:01.501
while they're creating it.

00:39:01.501 --> 00:39:05.291
Not just how to create a paper
or a project that gets an A,

00:39:05.291 --> 00:39:07.575
and it be judged only on
the intellectual merit,

00:39:07.575 --> 00:39:10.565
but just as much as you would
give a demerit for plagiarism,

00:39:10.565 --> 00:39:13.484
for example, or worse than a demerit,

00:39:13.484 --> 00:39:16.951
not taking care of data properly,

00:39:16.951 --> 00:39:19.049
file naming formats and things like that,

00:39:19.049 --> 00:39:21.811
is something that people
need to learn essentially

00:39:21.811 --> 00:39:23.617
from the cradle, because
it's not something

00:39:23.617 --> 00:39:27.687
that they're going to
intuit until it's too late.

00:39:27.687 --> 00:39:31.777
So finally let me just end
with a few exhortations

00:39:31.777 --> 00:39:33.485
to people particularly in the library,

00:39:33.485 --> 00:39:36.628
and particularly in preservation.

00:39:36.628 --> 00:39:39.193
I think that first I
want to emphasize again

00:39:39.193 --> 00:39:41.777
how crucial selection is.

00:39:41.777 --> 00:39:45.377
I think that archivists
have a unique perspective

00:39:45.377 --> 00:39:48.276
on dealing with abundance
and sorting through

00:39:48.276 --> 00:39:50.552
what's important to document,
what's important to save,

00:39:50.552 --> 00:39:52.043
what's important to sample.

00:39:52.043 --> 00:39:53.851
And I think audio visual archivists

00:39:53.851 --> 00:39:55.408
are particularly adept at this.

00:39:55.408 --> 00:39:58.515
I think we have a lot to learn from them.

00:39:58.515 --> 00:40:01.915
I also think that preservation is,

00:40:01.915 --> 00:40:04.216
and I know this will sound somewhat bold

00:40:04.216 --> 00:40:07.306
and even counter-intuitive
in these budgetary times,

00:40:07.306 --> 00:40:10.294
but I really do believe
passionately that preservation

00:40:10.294 --> 00:40:13.245
is emerging as a keystone
of the university

00:40:13.245 --> 00:40:14.995
and its social worth.

00:40:16.018 --> 00:40:18.051
I really think in this
time when universities

00:40:18.051 --> 00:40:21.382
feel besieged by so much, and
their whole social mission

00:40:21.382 --> 00:40:25.193
is under question from many
sectors, they can claim,

00:40:25.193 --> 00:40:27.623
rightly so, that they
contribute to knowledge,

00:40:27.623 --> 00:40:30.172
that they really contribute
to the knowledge economy.

00:40:30.172 --> 00:40:32.146
But so do a lot of other sectors.

00:40:32.146 --> 00:40:35.262
A lot of private sectors
contribute to knowledge as well.

00:40:35.262 --> 00:40:38.106
The one thing that the
private sector will never do.

00:40:38.106 --> 00:40:41.608
And this was demonstrated
roundly by our economists is,

00:40:41.608 --> 00:40:44.284
they will never care about preservation.

00:40:44.284 --> 00:40:46.845
They will never ensure the
continuity of information

00:40:46.845 --> 00:40:49.024
from one generation to another.

00:40:49.024 --> 00:40:50.741
It is impossible for market forces

00:40:50.741 --> 00:40:52.393
to actual take care of that.

00:40:52.393 --> 00:40:56.235
And so I think that, of all
the things that libraries do,

00:40:56.235 --> 00:40:57.443
on university campuses,

00:40:57.443 --> 00:41:01.788
I have heard people on
universities administration

00:41:01.788 --> 00:41:04.346
speculating how all of them could go away.

00:41:04.346 --> 00:41:07.668
Many of the things we
love most about libraries

00:41:07.668 --> 00:41:10.390
can very well be imagined
as being superseded

00:41:10.390 --> 00:41:12.393
by commercial services frankly.

00:41:12.393 --> 00:41:16.052
Search, for example, access for example.

00:41:16.052 --> 00:41:19.321
But no one believes that
anybody but a university

00:41:19.321 --> 00:41:20.910
is going to preserve.

00:41:20.910 --> 00:41:23.410
And so I think it means that

00:41:23.410 --> 00:41:25.714
this is really the golden
age of preservation.

00:41:25.714 --> 00:41:27.974
That if the university
can really configure

00:41:27.974 --> 00:41:31.368
a lot of its information
culture around the creation

00:41:31.368 --> 00:41:32.997
and curation of data,

00:41:32.997 --> 00:41:36.169
that the library really
stands to benefit enormously.

00:41:36.169 --> 00:41:40.522
It means a lot of changes in
the profession of preservation.

00:41:40.522 --> 00:41:42.679
It means working with a
lot of different skills

00:41:42.679 --> 00:41:45.294
and developing different
skillsets than we had before

00:41:45.294 --> 00:41:49.131
but I really believe that it
is, it really is the beginning

00:41:49.131 --> 00:41:52.501
of a marvelous age for preservation.

00:41:52.501 --> 00:41:54.633
Preservation, the paradigm preservation

00:41:54.633 --> 00:41:58.068
for the digital world is not about fixity,

00:41:58.068 --> 00:42:01.420
it's about incremental responsibilities.

00:42:01.420 --> 00:42:04.312
You can think long term
but really incrementally.

00:42:04.312 --> 00:42:08.123
The key is to think about
hand-offs, secure hand-offs.

00:42:08.123 --> 00:42:10.509
Making sure that data is
well curated and making sure

00:42:10.509 --> 00:42:14.384
that it is handed off to
somebody who can curate it

00:42:14.384 --> 00:42:17.048
and steward it in the next step.

00:42:17.048 --> 00:42:18.584
I think you have a lot of skills on campus

00:42:18.584 --> 00:42:19.979
that are very good at this.

00:42:19.979 --> 00:42:22.010
I think there are many
people at the Beinecke

00:42:22.010 --> 00:42:25.721
who know precisely how to
identify really important

00:42:25.721 --> 00:42:29.441
content creators, and
collectors of content.

00:42:29.441 --> 00:42:31.264
How to develop relationships with them

00:42:31.264 --> 00:42:34.494
and how to ensure that their
collection of information

00:42:34.494 --> 00:42:38.332
comes to the Beinecke when
they are ready to move it

00:42:38.332 --> 00:42:40.209
to a stewardship organization.

00:42:40.209 --> 00:42:44.413
Take that paradigm, move it
online, go to digital creators,

00:42:44.413 --> 00:42:48.775
and I think you have exactly
the same thing going on.

00:42:48.775 --> 00:42:51.099
What is it that makes this
obligation for preservation

00:42:51.099 --> 00:42:52.182
so important?

00:42:54.402 --> 00:42:57.625
I think it's important for
us, not just as individuals,

00:42:57.625 --> 00:43:00.654
and as families, and as
researchers at universities,

00:43:00.654 --> 00:43:03.582
but I think it is really
vital for us as a species,

00:43:03.582 --> 00:43:06.868
as homo sapiens, and I
mean that quite literally.

00:43:06.868 --> 00:43:09.724
We differ from our fellow
species in many ways.

00:43:09.724 --> 00:43:11.650
But scientists are telling us every day

00:43:11.650 --> 00:43:15.205
we differ a lot less from our
fellow species than we think.

00:43:15.205 --> 00:43:17.948
But there is one thing
incontestably we differ from

00:43:17.948 --> 00:43:20.682
all other species with,
and that is the ability

00:43:20.682 --> 00:43:23.118
to create artificial memory.

00:43:23.118 --> 00:43:26.894
To actually embody
knowledge in external forms

00:43:26.894 --> 00:43:30.474
that we then can pass on
across time, across space,

00:43:30.474 --> 00:43:32.446
and across multiple generations.

00:43:32.446 --> 00:43:34.888
We are the only species that does that,

00:43:34.888 --> 00:43:38.900
and we do it through preservation
so, I thank you very much.

00:43:38.900 --> 00:43:41.150
(applause)

00:43:48.185 --> 00:43:49.969
As you can tell I think
this is the beginning

00:43:49.969 --> 00:43:51.961
of a very exciting time for preservation

00:43:51.961 --> 00:43:56.083
and I know that I have
given a lot of information.

00:43:56.083 --> 00:43:59.317
Not nearly as much as I
would've liked to of course

00:43:59.317 --> 00:44:01.904
but I would to commend
to you the two reports

00:44:01.904 --> 00:44:05.471
of the task force and I
would simply, I would say

00:44:05.471 --> 00:44:09.765
just google sustainable
economics digital planet.

00:44:09.765 --> 00:44:13.765
That's a lot easier than
trying to find the URL.

00:44:14.730 --> 00:44:15.979
The reports are actually on

00:44:15.979 --> 00:44:18.729
the San Diego Supercomputer site.

00:44:19.886 --> 00:44:24.810
I also want to recommend to
you the Library of Congress

00:44:24.810 --> 00:44:28.977
has just published its 10 year
review of the ENDIP program

00:44:30.718 --> 00:44:34.801
and it's now online,
www.digitalpreservation.gov.

00:44:36.858 --> 00:44:38.771
And there's a full discussion about

00:44:38.771 --> 00:44:41.028
the many different models
of shared stewardship there

00:44:41.028 --> 00:44:42.022
that I commend to you.

00:44:42.022 --> 00:44:44.688
They're very frank discussions
of the various efforts

00:44:44.688 --> 00:44:48.855
that were tried some failed,
but in the end succeeded.

